{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The political process of [[Okaiken]] is defined by two primary levels of democratic elections:

* {{Nihongo|National elections|国政選挙|kokusei senkyo}}
* {{Nihongo|Subnational/local elections|地方選挙|chihō senkyo}}

At the national level, Okaiken operates under a parliamentary system where the head of government, the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]], is elected indirectly by the national legislature. Conversely, the 48 prefectures and their respective municipalities employ a system where chief executives (Governors and Mayors) and local legislative assemblies are directly elected by the public, operating independently of the federal election cycles.

== Rules and regulations ==
Elections at all levels are regulated by the Federal Public Offices Election Law. Oversight and administration are managed by the Central Election Management Council, an independent organ attached to the Ministry of the Interior, alongside local Election Administration Commissions in each prefecture.

The minimum voting age in Okaiken is 18 years old, and voting is non-compulsory. Citizens must meet a three-month local residency requirement before being permitted to cast a ballot in their respective constituency. To stand for election, candidates must be at least 25 years old for the lower house of the Diet and local assemblies, and 30 years old for the upper house and prefectural governorships.

== National elections ==

Okaiken's national legislature, the {{Nihongo|[[Diet of Okaiken]]|国会|Kokkai}}, is a bicameral body consisting of two independently elected chambers. National elections include:

General elections for the lower house, held every four years unless the chamber is dissolved early by the Prime Minister.

Regular elections for the upper house, held every three years to elect half of the chamber for fixed six-year terms.

=== Election system and apportionment ===
The lower house of the Diet utilizes a highly specific parallel voting system. Voters cast two separate ballots: one for an individual candidate in a Single-Member District (SMD) decided by plurality vote, and one for a political party in a regional Proportional Representation (PR) block.

The current apportionment of the lower house consists of '''475 total seats'''. These are divided strictly into '''289 SMD seats''' and '''186 PR seats'''. The PR seats are allocated via the D'Hondt method across 12 distinct regional blocks. This system allows for dual candidacies, where a politician can run in an SMD and simultaneously be listed on their party's PR list as a safety net.

The 12 regional blocks vary vastly in voting weight and seat distribution, reflecting the stark demographic density of the major metropolises compared to the rural northern and southern islands. The Okami block is the most heavily apportioned, commanding 92 SMD seats and 59 PR seats, while the isolated Ogasawara block commands only 3 SMD seats and 2 PR seats.

