=== Malapportionment and constitutional challenges ===
Like many rapidly urbanizing nations, Okaiken has historically struggled with malapportionment. During the economic booms of the late 20th century, massive demographic shifts saw rural populations migrate to the metropolitan hubs of Okami and Toyozawa. Because the electoral boundaries were not immediately redrawn, rural votes held significantly more weight than urban votes.

In recent decades, the Supreme Court of Okaiken has mandated aggressive redistricting to ensure that the maximum discrepancy in voting weight does not violate the constitutional principle of equality. The 2026 apportionment explicitly attempts to bridge this gap, though dense urban blocks like Okami (17.2 million population) and Miseto (9 million population) continue to dominate the raw seat count in the lower house.

== Subnational and local elections ==
Okaiken's local democratic process features elections for the 48 prefectural governorships and assemblies, as well as thousands of municipal mayoral and city council elections.

Many local elections are synchronized and held together during the {{Nihongo|unified local elections|統一地方選挙|Tōitsu chihō senkyo}}, which occur every four years. However, due to off-cycle resignations, historical municipal mergers, and political scandals that have forced early dissolutions, several major prefectures and cities operate on independent electoral calendars. In local elections, the voting methodology remains a single non-transferable vote (SNTV) for multi-member municipal assemblies, and a direct plurality vote for executive offices.

